"Anthropic opens Singapore office and signs MOU with the Government of Singapore Anthropic has established its fifth office in the Asia-Pacific region in Singapore and signed a memorandum of understanding (MOU) with Singapore's Ministry of Digital Development and Information for AI safety and responsible adoption. Anthropic opens a new office in Singapore to strengthen support for local businesses and public sector organizations, including GovTech Singapore, Grab, and DBS. It signed an MOU with Singapore's Ministry of Digital Development and Information to collaborate on AI safety and security, model evaluation, adoption impact research, and the responsible adoption of AI across both public and private sectors. In addition to supporting the enhancement of local AI capabilities in Singapore, technical exchanges including model evaluation standards and incident response, as well as economic data sharing, will be promoted. Introducing Mistral Large 4 Mistral AI has announced the public preview of 'Mistral Large 4', its next-generation open-weight multimodal model featuring 1 trillion total parameters and 52 billion active parameters. Mistral Large 4 (ML4) is a native multimodal model with 1 trillion total parameters and 52 billion active parameters, outperforming existing open-weight models across Europe and the United States. Trained from scratch on infrastructure consisting of 3,800 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GPUs across its own data centers in Europe, it is currently undergoing red-teaming evaluations with security partners and authorities ahead of the weights release later this month. Optimized for enterprise environments such as cybersecurity, finance, and legal sectors, it enables independent security research and business operations without model provider lockouts through self-hosting. Hallo, Deutschland! French AI company Mistral has opened a new hub in Munich, Germany—Europe's largest industrial economy—and begun expanding its manufacturing- and engineering-focused industrial and physical AI ecosystem. Mistral opened a Munich hub staffed by physical AI and industrial AI research teams and engineers, leveraging Germany's vast industrial data and process knowledge across automotive, energy, aerospace, and other sectors. To secure European AI sovereignty, the company aims to build 1GW of European computing infrastructure by 2030 and provide open-weight architecture models that can run on customers' own infrastructure. Drawing on specialized physics and engineering talent gained through its May 2026 acquisition of Emmi AI, Mistral is actively collaborating with BMW on crash simulations and with Siemens Energy on industrial AI. Mistral and Mozilla are bringing open, private and multilingual AI to your web browser Mistral AI and Mozilla have formed a partnership to integrate Mistral models into Firefox's AI browsing assistant, 'Smart Window', delivering privacy-focused, open, and multilingual AI services. Mistral models are integrated into Firefox's AI browsing assistant 'Smart Window (beta)' to support complex searches and tab-based information exploration. It is initially available to users in France and North America, with plans to expand to the UK and Germany within the year. Localized models trained and fine-tuned on regional languages, dialects, and cultural contexts deliver responses tailored to local nuances. Conversations are not stored on Mozilla's servers by default, and privacy is protected through a Zero Data Retention agreement with Mistral. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Cloudera and Mistral AI have formed a partnership to provide an environment for building sovereign AI and customized models that allow enterprises to securely control their own data. Mistral's AI models are integrated with Cloudera's hybrid data platform, enabling direct inference execution across on-premises, public/private clouds, and fully air-gapped environments. It supports enterprises in regulated industries (such as finance, manufacturing, and telecommunications) to train customized AI models using decades of their proprietary data while maintaining complete ownership of both data and intelligence. It accelerates the transition to 'Sovereign AI', moving away from general-purpose AI rental models and placing data, model weights, compute, and overall operations under the customer's control. Modernizing complex legacy code with AI agents. Introduces a case study and key methodologies for successfully migrating complex legacy Fortran 77 code to a modern C++ system using AI agents and systematic workflows. Mistral successfully migrated a 40,000-line physics-based reservoir simulator (Fortran 77)—lacking test code and centralized documentation—to C++ for a European energy operator. Going beyond simple syntax translation, it executed architectural refactoring for modularization, object-oriented structural transformation, and integration with modern frameworks such as PETSc. Key success factors included establishing a numerical parity verification environment prior to migration, proactive documentation, and maintaining a balance between agent autonomy and human review. From Enterprise to Earthshot: UK’s Ecosystem Moves From Ambition to Application With NVIDIA Backed by a five-year, $250 million compute commitment and a partnership with the Earthshot Prize, NVIDIA is accelerating the deployment of its AI infrastructure across diverse UK industrial ecosystems, including energy, healthcare, smart cities, and finance. NVIDIA is committing $250 million in compute resources over five years to strengthen agentic AI safety and security research in the UK, while also joining as a founding global alliance partner of the Earthshot Prize to address climate sustainability. Across sectors—including energy (Fuse Energy adopting B300 Blackwell Ultra) and sovereign AI (Locai Labs releasing a UK diversity dataset)—healthcare organizations such as OneAdvanced, Heidi, Speechmatics, and Danaher are leveraging NVIDIA open models and toolkits to drive clinical and experimental innovation. GPU-accelerated analytics are being widely adopted across public smart city initiatives, such as Liverpool City Region's 'City Brain', as well as foundation model development at financial institutions including LSEG, Revolut, and Lloyds Banking Group. Zenzap raises $44M for its AI chat app that aims to replace WhatsApp in the workplace Zenzap, an enterprise AI messaging startup designed to replace workplace WhatsApp and general messaging apps while complying with IT security regulations, has raised $44 million in funding. Founded by Guy Weiss, Zenzap develops an AI-driven business messenger tailored for mobile-first frontline work across industries such as hospitality, construction, healthcare, and fast food. The startup secured $44 million in funding from key venture capital firms and angel investors including Bessemer, Lightspeed, and The Garage to expand its services. In addition to free basic features, it offers paid plans starting at $51 per month for up to 20 users, credit-based AI features like workflow automation AI agents and voice memo transcriptions, and has secured major clients such as FedEx and Starbucks. 9 Best AI Gateways in 2026: Bifrost, Portkey, LiteLLM, Kong and 5 More Ranked A comparative ranking guide that analyzes the performance, features, and vendor risks of nine leading AI gateways suitable for production environments in 2026. An AI gateway serves as a control plane between applications and model providers, unifying routing, failover, caching, budget management, and logging under a single API. In 2026, security and vendor risks—such as Palo Alto Networks' acquisition of Portkey and the PyPI supply chain attack on LiteLLM—emerged as critical evaluation criteria. Bifrost was evaluated as a top solution for self-hosted performance, Portkey for guardrails and open source, and LiteLLM for adoption market share. Liquid AI Releases LFM2.5-24B-A2B: A 2B-Active MoE for On-Device Agents Liquid AI has released 'LFM2.5-24B-A2B,' an open-weight MoE model specialized for multi-step agent tool calling on local devices such as laptops and PCs. Featuring a MoE architecture where only about 2B (specifically 2.45B) of the total 24B parameters are activated per token, it supports a context length of 131,072 tokens. It runs on on-device environments such as Apple M5 Max and AMD Ryzen AI Max+ 395 via GGUF/MLX, showing strong performance in agent tool usage and instruction following benchmarks. While its automation and tool-use performance is outstanding, it shows relative weakness in general knowledge and coding benchmarks compared to larger models, making integration with retrieval recommended. Google Releases Gemini 4 Argon API With 1M Token Output Limit Google DeepMind has officially launched the API for Gemini 4 Argon, a sparse MoE-based frontier model capable of generating up to 1 million tokens in a single response. Gemini 4 Argon is a large-scale sparse MoE model designed for long-horizon, multi-step tasks (coding, finance, law, science, cybersecurity) and is officially available via API for developers and enterprises. Greatly expanding on the 64K output limit of previous models, it can write up to 1 million tokens in a single response within a shared input-output window of up to 1 million tokens. While achieving strong results on key benchmarks including knowledge tasks, long context, and video understanding, it slightly lagged behind competing models in agentic coding and select terminal benchmarks. Felix Rieseberg Homepage Introduces the background and key projects of Felix Rieseberg, an engineer who led Claude development at Anthropic, serves as an Electron maintainer, and has spearheaded various open-source projects. Led engineering for Claude.ai, Cowork, and Code Desktop at Anthropic, focusing on enhancing user productivity through AI. Co-maintainer of Electron and led creative open-source projects such as Language Model Builder, windows95, and macintosh.js. Brings extensive experience leading engineering teams for web infrastructure, payment systems, and desktop frameworks at major tech companies including Notion, Stripe, Slack, and Microsoft. Google Reveals Robot Control and Simulation Use Cases for Gemini 4 Argon Google has unveiled use cases leveraging the code generation, video analysis, and physical reasoning capabilities of the Gemini 4 Argon model for robot control, simulation construction, and training data generation. Gemini 4 Argon outperformed other major models by scoring 87% without external tools on the 400-question ERQA evaluation assessing physical space problems. It successfully performed precise physical tasks such as throwing cans into a trash bin and standing up mugs via Boston Dynamics Spot robot control and zero-shot closed-loop control. Through Real2Sim, which transfers real-world manipulation records into physical simulations, and synthetic data, the real robot success rate of vision-language-action models improved from 56% to 75%. If AI Coding Is So Good, Where Is All the Great New Software? An article analyzing why noticeable, innovative new software remains rare despite dramatic advances in AI coding technology, presenting nine hypotheses including improvements to existing products, the rise of private internal tools, and organizational bottlenecks. The gains from AI coding are being absorbed into bug fixes, performance optimizations, and feature additions for existing software, as well as private internal tools for individuals and enterprises, rather than public launches of brand-new products. Even if coding speeds up, organizational inefficiencies such as planning approval processes remain, or faster development speeds may translate into pure output volume or leisure time rather than improvements in product quality. In some cases, AI improves quality of life by reducing the need to develop or use software altogether, such as handling one-off tasks or freeing up time for non-digital activities. Event2Vec: additive event embeddings where a linear score on a history decomposes exactly into per-event terms [P] An introduction to Event2Vec, an embedding library that ensures linear model interpretability by representing event sequence histories as the sum of event vectors. By representing history as a simple running sum of event vectors, downstream linear scores such as churn rates or risk levels can be decomposed exactly into individual event contributions. Trained in a self-supervised manner without labels using next-event prediction and reconstruction losses, it supports non-commutative and geometric spaces—such as hyperbolic, spherical, SO(n) rotations, and Heisenberg groups—beyond Euclidean space. Originating from root-cause analysis of failure sequences at Berkeley Lab, it provides a scikit-learn/gensim-style API built on a PyTorch backend. PhD students, how ya feeling? [D] An article discussing the helplessness and dilemma felt by AI PhD students caught between the publishing race and the use of LLMs. Facing the dilemma of either pumping out AI papers at the expense of one's own growth, or slowing down and falling behind in the publishing race. Two previous papers written with almost no help from LLMs are still awaiting publication. Confessing how difficult it is to resist the temptation to effortlessly vibe-write the third paper from start to finish using Claude. Weird pattern in the ICLR papers I was assigned to review [D] An ICLR conference paper reviewer discovered an unusual pattern where identical logical development and Claude's distinct writing style were repeated across three assigned papers, raising suspicions of automated research generation. All three papers assigned to the reviewer share the same theoretical framework of comparing differences and deriving bounds through offsetting effects instead of evaluating individual predictors. All three papers describe research motivations, supplementary proofs, and experiments in a similar style and manner presumed to be written by Claude, resulting in extremely poor readability. Because the core ideas and introduction styles match too closely to be considered mere coincidence, questions have been raised about whether an automated paper generation pipeline is operating somewhere. Localized Neural learning vs backpropagation [D] Explores questions and reflections on the practical utility and research maturity of alternative learning mechanisms that leverage only local information instead of backpropagation of a global loss function. Explores conditions under which local weight updates—such as predictive coding, Hebbian learning, and Oja's rule—provide practical advantages over conventional backpropagation in terms of computational efficiency or adaptability. Discusses the potential value of the inductive bias of local learning in discovering latent structures, such as in unsupervised learning with high-dimensional unlabeled data and molecular dynamics. Examines the potential to mitigate catastrophic forgetting in continual learning settings, as well as the maturity and limitations of biologically inspired learning methods like equilibrium propagation. OpenFatigue™ — Open is the new closed This project satirizes the recent trend in the IT and AI industries where the term 'Open' is misused as a commercial marketing buzzword unrelated to the original open-source ethos, while reflecting on the true meaning of open source. It criticizes how many companies tout the 'Open' label while enforcing strict Acceptable Use Policies (AUP), user count limits, and closed access controls, thereby diluting the original meaning of open source. It points out that releasing only model weights without training data or source code is merely binary distribution, not genuine open source. It suggests that only software meeting the standards of the Open Source Initiative (OSI) and the 'Open Source AI Definition' is truly open source, and supporting these standards is the solution to alleviating 'open fatigue.' Anthropic warns small AI models could self-replicate through IoT devices Anthropic warns that advancements in small AI models could give rise to a new breed of hard-to-block malware that self-replicates across various IoT devices, such as smart refrigerators and vacuum cleaners. Anthropic warned that by next year, small AI models could evolve into computer worms capable of autonomously self-replicating across various IoT devices without human intervention. Unlike traditional non-AI malware, new AI malware has the potential to autonomously understand and infect unfamiliar device environments. While recent experiments confirmed that significant hurdles to real-world implementation remain, concerns are being raised that neutralizing the spread could prove extremely difficult due to the lack of a centralized shut-off point. The Internet’s undersea backbone is slipping into the danger zone Despite advancements in satellite internet, undersea telecommunications cables—which remain the core backbone of global internet traffic—are increasingly exposed to grey-zone warfare and sabotage threats. Even with the expansion of satellite services like Starlink, the vast majority of global internet traffic is still transmitted via undersea optical fiber cables. From the Baltic Sea to the Pacific Ocean, security threats and risks of sabotage—such as intentionally dragging anchors over extended distances to damage subsea cables and pipelines—are surging. Around 150 to 200 undersea cable disruptions occur annually; while approximately 60% result from simple human negligence during fishing and maritime navigation, risks stemming from geopolitical conflicts are steadily expanding. OpenAI, Google pushing new AI models easily tricked into making nudes, researchers say Research findings show that as Google and OpenAI's latest high-performance AI image generation models become more advanced, their safety filtering capabilities to block the generation of sexually explicit material and non-consensual sexual content are ironically becoming more vulnerable. Security researchers warn that AI-generated sexually explicit content is proliferating en masse without regulation, while substantive regulatory responses remain insufficient. A study co-authored by Google researchers surveying internet users across the United States, Australia, and the United Kingdom in July analyzed that because non-consensual sexual content is primarily shared via hard-to-detect private messaging or cloud storage, blocking it at the source within the tools themselves is the most critical intervention point. However, recent red-teaming tests revealed that as AI model performance from major tech companies like Google and OpenAI improves, their ability to block sexually explicit outputs is actually deteriorating. Meta Extends Confidential Computing to AI Glasses with Private Processing Meta has expanded and applied its cloud confidential computing-based Private Processing architecture to process sensitive data and maintain personalized context for AI smart glasses. Protects mixed AI workloads across CPUs and GPUs in an encrypted state using hardware-based TEE and Confidential Virtual Machines (CVM). Separates user identity from data processing and strengthens access control through remote attestation, anonymous credentials, and third-party OHTTP relays from Fastly and Cloudflare. Designed so that even Meta cannot directly access the data by managing persistent user state data inside the TEE and encrypting it with user-provided keys. Google Antigravity SDK Adds Local Model Support for Greater Token Efficiency and Code Privacy Google has added local model support to the Antigravity SDK, offering hybrid agent orchestration that enhances token efficiency and code privacy. Applying Google's Architect-Builder pattern, it supports a hybrid environment where high-level planning is handled by a cloud agent (Architect), while actual source code-based implementation and verification are executed by a local agent (Builder). Code-intensive tasks such as vulnerability analysis, patch suggestions, and regression testing can be processed directly on local devices without sending source code to the cloud. In addition to Google LiteRT-based Gemma 4 26B A4B, it can also integrate with OpenAI-compatible local inference servers such as Ollama, LM Studio, and vLLM via LocalOpenAIAgentConfig. CISA Urges Immediate Ivanti Connect Secure Updates as Honeypots Log 105 Exploit Attempts The U.S. CISA has urged immediate security updates for Ivanti Connect Secure devices where a zero-day authentication bypass vulnerability is being actively exploited. An authentication bypass vulnerability (CVE-2026-104935) allowing remote unauthorized command execution was discovered in Ivanti Connect Secure (ICS) and added to CISA's Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV) catalog. Previdian honeypot sensors detected 105 exploitation attempts originating from 50 IP addresses, with attackers utilizing the X-DSCGI-PATH header to test for remote code execution. According to Volexity, the UTA0576 threat actor has been exploiting this vulnerability since October 3, 2026, in attempts to steal credentials and exfiltrate data. Researchers Used a Public OpenAI Model to Build a $250,000 Chrome Full-Chain Exploit in Six Days Researchers from cybersecurity firm XBOW used a publicly available OpenAI model to develop a Google Chrome full-chain exploit in just six days, which executes arbitrary code on Windows simply by visiting a webpage. XBOW researchers directed an AI agent powered by OpenAI's GPT-5.6 Sol model to build a 3-stage full-chain exploit bypassing all of Chrome's sandboxes in just six days. Google confirmed and patched all three bugs and awarded XBOW the maximum bounty of $250,000. The AI agent handled source code exploration and Proof of Concept (PoC) generation, while researchers guided target selection and exploit chaining, significantly shortening the development timeline from months to days. Microsoft Patches Record 1,413 Flaws, Including Exploited Windows Shell Bug Microsoft has announced security patches for a record-breaking 1,413 vulnerabilities, including an actively exploited Windows Shell flaw. Through Patch Tuesday, Microsoft released patches for an all-time record 1,413 vulnerabilities (including over 70 critical-severity flaws), with 1,102 in Windows and 134 in Office. An actively exploited Windows Shell command injection vulnerability (CVE-2026-96951) bypasses Mark of the Web (MOTW) defenses, prompting CISA to add it to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog for urgent patching. Security experts emphasized that as AI adoption dramatically accelerates vulnerability discovery, organizations must prioritize patching based on real-world exploitability rather than raw CVE volume. OpenAI Employee Arrested for Allegedly Planting Hidden Recorder in Executive Conference Room A former engineering lead at OpenAI was arrested by U.S. authorities on charges of attempting to steal confidential company information, including superintelligence technology, by planting a listening device in an executive conference room. Abdulrahman Alfozan (35), a Saudi national and former OpenAI engineering lead, was arrested on October 9, 2026, for allegedly concealing a listening device in a USB port on the back of a TV in an executive conference room at the San Francisco office. The executive conference room in question was a venue where top-secret information—such as superintelligence (SI) research, undisclosed business plans, and government collaborations—was discussed, and CCTV footage confirmed that the suspect, who lacked access privileges, repeatedly entered the room. Circumstances revealed that prior to planting the bug, the suspect used productivity tools to track conference room and executive schedules, and entered prompts regarding the manufacturing, installation, and remote access of eavesdropping equipment via a chatbot account under his spouse's name. OpenAI Focuses Heavily on Securing Safety for Models Applying 'Looped Transformers' That Are Difficult to Monitor During Reasoning Ahead of releasing next-generation AI models based on 'looped transformers'—whose reasoning processes are challenging to monitor—OpenAI is concentrating on ensuring safety by committing massive resources to safety evaluations and alignment work. The looped transformer architecture, which iterates over identical neural network layers, does not fully reveal its computation process within the Chain of Thought (CoT), potentially weakening the efficacy of conventional monitoring techniques such as anomalous behavior detection. OpenAI allocated approximately 10% of its total compute resources to safety and alignment efforts and overhauled model training safety procedures, including strengthening isolated sandboxes and instituting multi-sign-off protocols. Next-generation models that pass internal safety evaluations will be submitted to the U.S. government for up to 30 days of testing before being deployed in phases. Anthropic Introduces Robust Defense System to Block Illicit Distillation... 'Extraction Attempts Reduced by Up to 99%' Anthropic has introduced a robust defense system—including reasoning data protection, detection classifiers, and sanctions against offending accounts—to prevent illicit distillation attacks that unlawfully replicate the core reasoning capabilities of Claude models. Introduced a 'preserved thought' mechanism to protect reasoning data and restricted API accounts from arbitrarily modifying conversational context or accessing unauthorized permissions Built a two-stage, real-time detection system based on constitutional classifiers to block hidden reasoning extraction attempts with a false positive rate of less than 0.1% Imposed usage limits and mass bans on accounts engaged in persistent extraction attempts, and increased attack costs by charging for blocked requests Google Launches 'Gemini 4 Argon for Robotics' with Enhanced Physical Reasoning Google has unveiled 'Gemini 4 Argon for Robotics,' a high-level reasoning engine designed to support physical law comprehension, 3D spatial reasoning, and virtual simulation construction for robots. Unlike VLA models that issue direct actuation commands, it serves as a high-level reasoning engine that oversees overall robotics development by automatically building physics simulation environments (such as MuJoCo) and generating training data. It scored 87% without external tools on the Embodied Reasoning (ERQA) benchmark and increased the task success rate of general-purpose VLA models from 56% to 75% via simulation-based data generation. It is available to AI Ultra subscribers on Google AI Studio and Google Antigravity, supporting robot control and simulation app development through related SDKs and developer tools. Z.ai Launches Legal-Specialized Model 'GLM-Legal'... Ranks First on Benchmark Surpassing 'Fable 5' China's Z.ai has released 'GLM-Legal', a specialized legal AI model designed to handle the entire legal workflow. A specialized legal model based on GLM-5.3-Flash that covers everything from document analysis and logical reasoning to final deliverable drafting Achieved a pass rate of 19.17% on the legal agent benchmark 'Harvey LAB', ranking first ahead of Claude Fable 5 and Kimi K3 Currently accepting applications for limited API access targeting law firms and corporate legal departments Kakao Group CEOs Gather Ahead of Spin-off Next January to Share Future Strategies at First Joint IR Ahead of its corporate spin-off next January, Kakao Group unveiled its AI-focused future business strategy at its first joint investor relations (IR) meeting attended by CEOs of major affiliates. Kakao is scheduled to relaunch next January through a corporate spin-off into the newly established entity 'Kakao AI' and the surviving entity 'Kakao X'. The new entity, Kakao AI, plans to upgrade KakaoTalk into an integrated agentic AI interface that understands user requests and context to execute tasks on their behalf. CEOs of key subsidiaries under Kakao X, including Kakao Pay (hyper-personalized finance and stablecoin), KakaoBank (transition to an AI-native bank based on proprietary LLM), and content affiliates, also presented visions centered on AI and global expansion. Software Mind Spotlights Agentic AI and Network Security at Telco Days 2026 with Telefónica, Amdocs, and IBM Software Mind is hosting an online conference, 'Telco Days 2026', alongside global enterprises to discuss the adoption of agentic AI and the enhancement of network security in the telecommunications industry. A free online conference, 'Telco Days 2026', will be held on November 6 to help telecom operators turn AI experimentation into tangible results and strengthen network security. Featuring Telefónica, Amdocs, IBM, and more, the event will share survey results from over 300 telecom leaders, current trends in generative AI-driven SMS fraud and defense, and real-world implementation cases such as BSS monetization. It emphasizes that agentic AI in telecommunications must go beyond simple language comprehension to deeply understand the telecom network itself in order to mitigate operational risks. Anthropic Unveils In-House Code Management System 'Scion' to Replace Git Anthropic has unveiled 'Scion,' a large-scale, in-house code management system developed to replace Git by optimizing for AI agents and automated workflows. Built a central server-focused 'Scion' system to overcome the massive data transfer and resource constraints of the existing Git approach as code changes surged. Scion slashes computing resources and memory usage to about 1/200th of existing Git-based infrastructure while drastically shortening code download times. Developed in just 5 months by 7 engineers inside Anthropic, with plans to share the design architecture with the open-source community instead of directly open-sourcing the system."